<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ludovico Antonio Muratori</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1672">1672</a>–<a href="/years/1750">1750</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ludovico Antonio Muratori (1672–1750) was an Italian priest and historian, librarian of the Este dukes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> from 1700. His Rerum Italicarum Scriptores collected the sources of medieval Italian history and laid the foundations for its critical study. He also discovered the Muratorian fragment, the oldest known list of books of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ludovico-antonio-muratori.jpg" alt="Ludovico Antonio Muratori" width="320" height="371" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giovanni_antonio_cybei,_ritratto_di_ludovico_antonio_muratori,_1774_(modena,_biblioteca_estense_universitaria).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
